{"hash":"e8153fa8e5cfef9c","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":78.48,"said":"Here on the sea wall stands the Baan Stambh, the Arrow Pillar. Look at the top: a globe, pierced by an arrow pointing due south, out over the open water. The Sanskrit words on it say ‘Aasamudrant Dakshin Dhruv Paryant Abaadhit Jyotirmarg’: ‘from this shore to the South Pole, an unobstructed path of light’. They carry an old belief, that if you sailed due south from Somnath you would meet no land until the far end of the earth. How old is the idea? Nobody can say for certain. Popular accounts call it centuries old, but no firm record tells us when it was first written, and the pillar you see stands on the temple’s modern sea wall. What it keeps alive is a feeling: Somnath as a lamp at the edge of the land, facing the endless ocean, on the same shore where the Moon once found his light again. Stand here and listen to the waves. They have washed this shore through every chapter of Somnath’s story, the golden days and the broken ones. Now turn around and face the temple, because those chapters are written in its stones.\n\nDid you know? Somnath is the first name in the Dwadasha Jyotirlinga Stotram: ‘Saurashtre Somanatham cha’, ‘Somanatha in Saurashtra’.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1031},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1047,"end":1165}],"words":[[0.098,0.397,0,4],[0.507,0.12,5,7],[0.639,0.096,8,11],[0.747,0.252,12,15],[1.012,0.325,16,20],[1.348,0.445,21,27],[1.805,0.096,28,31],[1.913,0.325,32,36],[2.249,0.457,37,43],[2.923,0.156,45,48],[3.091,0.361,49,54],[3.463,0.409,55,61],[4.772,0.228,63,67],[5.012,0.12,68,70],[5.144,0.096,71,74],[5.252,0.373,75,78],[5.805,0.06,80,81],[5.877,0.445,82,87],[6.526,0.385,89,96],[6.923,0.192,97,99],[7.127,0.216,100,102],[7.356,0.325,103,108],[7.692,0.517,109,117],[8.221,0.192,118,121],[8.425,0.493,122,127],[9.099,0.228,129,132],[9.339,0.24,133,137],[9.591,0.108,138,141],[9.712,0.361,142,146],[10.084,0.469,147,152],[11.43,0.108,154,